; =====================================================================
; cddl-fragments-dedented.derived.cddl
;
; DERIVED -- NOT verbatim. This is cddl-fragments.cddl with the RFC's
; three-space left margin removed so that a CDDL tool can read it. The
; text inside each region is otherwise untouched, and the removal
; round-trips: re-indenting every non-blank line by three spaces
; reproduces the verbatim file's regions exactly.
;
; DO NOT CONCATENATE THE REGIONS -- F02 and F03 define my-sequence
; differently. See cddl-fragments.cddl.
;
; None of these three rules is a rule for a CBOR Sequence. They are the
; document's own fragments; two of them are ARRAY rules standing in for
; a sequence rule the language cannot write (§4.1). What a schema of
; ours would say is in cbor-seq-container.derived.cddl.
;
; Source: RFC 8742, cached source sha256
;   4462966836b60f42ebae9b5f1d2a479c328e16f0380a3434311a083bccbce30e
; =====================================================================

; --- F01  §4.1   dedented from source lines 195-197
my-embedded-cbor-seq = bytes .cborseq my-array
my-array = [* my-element]
my-element = my-foo / my-bar

; --- F02  §4.1   dedented from source lines 206-209
; This defines an array, the elements of which are to be used
; in a CBOR Sequence:
my-sequence = [* my-element]
my-element = my-foo / my-bar

; --- F03  §4.3   dedented from source lines 245-249
; This defines an array of CBOR byte strings, the elements of which
; are to be used in a CBOR Sequence:
my-sequence = [* my-element]
my-element = bytes .cbor my-element-structure
my-element-structure = my-foo / my-bar

